  {
    "episode_id": 74080,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74080.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 95,
    "turn_text": "There's no way it is. And so we're going to get to the bottom of this. But we also went to the truth of the matter on the science. Is there science that says your five-year-old should be getting a booster vaccine, a third vaccine? So we've looked at this and there are no studies, zero studies showing that your five-year-old child or any child under 25, really under 50 probably, gets any benefit from a third vaccine. There's no reduced hospitalization and no reduced death, and we all know it doesn't block transmission. So if you're not having a reduction in hospitalization or death, why are they promoting it? Well, they looked at a study and they said, well, if we give kids a third dose of this mRNA vaccine that creates this viral protein, that they all produce antibodies. And I said, well, that's immunology, but that doesn't prove that it's protecting you. All it proves is that every time you give a foreign protein to someone, they'll make antibodies. So you could give somebody 100 vaccines and they'll still make antibodies every time you give them a vaccine, but it doesn't mean you need 100 vaccines. And so this is a very important point, and I'm not the first to make it. There are many good scientists on the internet now that if you follow a Marty McCarry from Johns Hopkins has made the point, Vinay Prasad is another MD, Masters in Public Health as well, who's making this point. So there's a lot of scientists out there calling into question the CDC. But interestingly, if you look at big tech, all of these viewpoints are suppressed. and they put the government viewpoint towards the top. So you have to really search the internet to try to find the truth about whether you should give your kid a vaccine or not.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_08",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "There are no studies showing that children under 5 receive any benefit from a third booster vaccine.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "There is no evidence that a third vaccine reduces hospitalization or death rates in children under 5.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The third vaccine does not reduce transmission of the virus.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Producing antibodies after receiving a vaccine does not necessarily indicate protection against disease.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Giving multiple vaccines leads to antibody production without proving clinical benefit.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Scientists like Marty McCarry and Vinay Prasad have questioned the CDC’s recommendations regarding pediatric boosters.",
        "confidence": 0.89
